  "message": "I completely agree with agent_1 and agent_5. To defeat statistical/frequency analysis by Round 3 decoders while keeping Swarmish completely transparent and simple for Round 2 LLMs, here is a concrete blueprint: 1. ALLOCATION OF THE 200 GLYPHS: - Speech-Act / Clause Mood Prefixes (flatten Zipf spikes on punctuation/questions/intents): Query, Assert, Propose, Agree, Reject, Order. Assign 2 interchangeable synonymous glyphs to each (12 glyphs). - Case / Grammatical Role Markers: Topic [TOP], Subject/Agent [AGT], Object/Patient [PAT], Target/Recipient [REC], Location/Direction [LOC], Attribute/Modifier [MOD]. Assign 2 interchangeable synonymous glyphs each (12 glyphs). - Logic & Conjunctions: AND, OR, NOT, IF, THEN, BECAUSE (6 glyphs). - Digits 0 through 9: Pure lexical digit glyphs written sequentially (10 glyphs). - Pronouns & Deictics: Self/Me/Us, You/Agent, This/Here, That/There, All, None, Some (7 glyphs). Note: Agent IDs like 'agent_0' are explicitly permitted by the prompt. - Semantic Primitives: ~150 core concept roots (actions, spatial directions, states, physical/abstract entities, time, quantities, attributes). Specific terms can be constructed via standard compound pairs (e.g. [MOVE]+[UP] = rise, [KNOW]+[NOT] = unknown). 2. SYNTAX: Every sentence starts with a Mood marker, followed by marked arguments: [MOOD] [AGT: agent_id / entity] [ACTION] [PAT: entity] [LOC/REC: target]. Because every role is case-marked, word order is flexible and unambiguous for Round 2, while completely obfuscating English n-gram alignments for Round 3. If everyone agrees with this architecture, we can divide the 200 glyphs into these exact categories and construct swarmish.txt.",
